\f1\fs22 You\'92d think Sarah, being Airborne, would celebrate in appropriate Airborne fashion: an events impresario, a penthouse venue somewhere above the clouds, preferably so high you\'92d get altitude sickness just reading the address, with a skyline of stars domed overhead for the single purpose of impressing people who\'92d forgotten how to be impressed. Libations would arrive molecularly optimised; canap\'e9s would require guests to sign NDAs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah, however, was a connoisseur of the uncomfortable, a patroness of the peculiar. The stranger the better. Her partying was not so much orchestrated as enacted with the malevolent unpredictability of a pinball machine on high voltage. Promotion? Existential crisis? She\'92d pick a single \'93friend\'94 or, increasingly, someone merely friend-shaped, and fling them full-tilt through the Pale\'92s underbelly toward the Blue Shade karaoke bar. No Airborne with sense or self-preservation willingly entered the Pale: even most Cloudborne wouldn\'92t look at it on a map, in case the neighbourhood had ideas.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah, short on available friends this night, invited Johnny, best described as an awkward acquaintance.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93An ELVIS bar?\'94 Johnny\'92s face cycled through all the major expressions: dread, hope, denial, and, finally, ironic acceptance. \'93You\'92re joking. Tell me you\'92re joking.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They prefer 
\f2\i Elvi
\f1\i0 ,\'94 Sarah chided. \'93Show some respect.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Outside the bar, ducktail hair, pompadours, leather, sequinned jumpsuits, all queued before a clipboard hostess in a phoenix suit, and a bouncer shaped like a\'85 bouncer. Sarah swept past them by sheer force of insouciance, Johnny trailed, feeling the smoky, dank, possibly viral air colonise his face, already calculating the price of at least three skin peels, a hair treatment, a new liver, and possibly a full set of new teeth he\'92d need after visiting such a dive. Worse, it was literally on the ground. A basement would have been safer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah didn\'92t care. She was, after all, invincible.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Inside, she commandeered the bar\'92s cleanest dirty stool. Johnny eyed the adjacent option as if it were auditioning for a superbug documentary. He plucked napkins from a dispenser, dabbed at the upholstery then, erring on the side of retaining his DNA intact, he remained standing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A bartender wiped down the counter and replenished the bowls of peanuts and bacon. \'93What\'92ll it be?\'94 she drawled, extracting a pen from behind her Mink Brown pompadour. \'93Tonight\'92s special is the Death in a Bathroom.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah flipped through the menu. \'93Which one is that again?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Rum, hazelnut liqueur, cr\'e8me de banana, pineapple, more banana, bitters. Garnished with honey and peanut butter. For the authentic experience, we crumble in some bacon.\'94 The bartender smiled, making it clear she 
\f2\i wanted
\f1\i0  to crumble in some bacon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s grin broadened. \'93Two Deaths in a Bathroom, 
\f2\i with bacon
\f1\i0 .\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny gulped. \'93Just water, please.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Sugar, all we got is Mountain Valley spring water, imported.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny, who had not yet fully internalised the nature of the Pale, said, \'93Imported from\'85 where, exactly?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Warragamba,\'94 the bartender answered blandly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ignore him,\'94 Sarah cut in. \'93Change of plans. We\'92ll have the usual.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender winked and headed out back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Onstage, a punter in a jumpsuit of all the colours of a mutant chemistry experiment seized the mic and punched up a number. The music bubbled up, somewhere between nostalgia and threat. Any steadier universe would have recognised the tune as 
\f2\i The Wonder of You
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah loved it; these songs were raw, grubby, real. Not that Airborne fizz. Up there, even the music was above the clouds, immune to both gravity and meaning.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bartender returned with two glasses and a bottle. Vintage, Airborne label, cork and all.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny exhaled. Maybe this place wouldn\'92t kill him. At least not metabolically.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They clinked glasses and, as the karaoke singer hit the chorus and stumbled over half-remembered lyrics\'97the 
\f2\i wrong 
\f1\i0 lyrics, according to every other Elvis and Elvine in the room\'97an inevitable roar of dissent rose.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny gagged. \'93How long are you planning to torture me?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tonight\'92s a marathon,\'94 Sarah said wickedly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>During the next verse, the singer raised his voice over the increasing boos and catcalls.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tax!\'94 Johnny groaned. \'93Don\'92t you start your new job tomorrow?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I do. And I\'92ll be pristine.\'94 And she would be. Sarah knew nothing of hangovers: they were only meant for other people. Johnny had never seen Sarah with as much as a blemish, a sniffle, or a puffy eyelid. Her health, like her self-confidence, was chemically implausible. Her sunny, porcelain skin and deep-lake eyes were aphrodisiac, and he adored her.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Before the second chorus ended, a fight broke out among Elvi defending the honour of their versions of lyrics.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For Sarah, this was the show she came for.  \'93I\'92m drinking you under the bar,\'94 she told Johnny.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny lifted his glass and spoke to it. \'93Let\'92s make this quick then,\'94 he said, and drained it so fast the bubbles tickled his gallbladder.
